+## 6. A uniform outer-axis factorization
+
+The same grading gives a useful additional check.  On the coordinate axis
+`b3=b4=u_2=...=u_(t-2)=0`, put `z=u_(t-1)`,
+`s=z/X^(t-1)` and `Delta_n=n-(t-1)Theta`.  For `t>=5`, the only high
+coefficients whose weights are multiples of `t-1` are `chi_1` (the
+`c_(t-1)` solve) and `upsilon_2` (the `u_(2t-2)` solve); `b2` does not occur.
+After those two audited pivots, the first terminal term is
+
+```text
+[X^(t+4)]R=lambda_(t,t-1) u_(t-1)^3.                (6.1)
+```
+
+Writing `lambda=a(t)d+b(t)`, direct symbolic pair arithmetic factors its norm
+as
+
+```text
+3b(t)^2-(t+1)a(t)^2
+= 9t^2(t-1)^6(t+1)(t+2)^6(3t+1)^2(4t+1)
+    *(25t^2+12t-12)
+  /[(2t+1)^6(t^2+3t+6)
+    *(27t^4+206t^3+527t^2+540t+300)^3].             (6.2)
+```
+
+Every denominator in (6.2) is positive for `t>=5`, as is every numerator
+factor.  The intermediate divisions are only by `y`, `t-(t-1)=1`, odd Euler
+scalars, `p_C(t,t-1)`, and `p_Q(t,2t-2)`, all covered by Section 2.
+Consequently `lambda_(t,t-1)` is a unit of `A_t` for every integer `t>=5`,
+including both factors of every split algebra.  Exact coefficient-pair runs
+at `t=5,...,15` agree with (6.2); direct authenticated terminal records agree
+at `t=5`, and the generalized fixed-axis recurrence agrees at `t=6`.
+
+At the exceptional schedule sizes `t=3,4`, direct exact pair calculations
+(including the `b2` solve at `t=4`) also give nonzero norms.  Hence the image
+of `I_(t,+)` on the `u_(t-1)` coordinate axis has radical `(u_(t-1))` for
+every `t>=3`.  As in (4.7), this is not a full-ideal containment.
